#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e052cb8a-7337-57ef-99b4-65a1c742c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-620" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 12t+t^2, t^3+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12+2t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "88214928-ff75-51ac-bb97-f04b1f45360b" :question/math-academy-id "e-621" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 4t^2+1, 3t^2-t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector. We can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,6t-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c01d6202-76ac-5a5c-850f-5b699d01c462" :question/math-academy-id "e-7224" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm s,$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t^4-t^2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFind the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3}-2(1),6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(1)|\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨2,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "b7bffc21-d43c-5b2b-b09b-649d17b1eb96" :question/math-academy-id "e-7225" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r},$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle t^{5}-t^2,\\, t^2+6t-3 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4}-2t,\\,2t+6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨20{t}^{3}-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨20(0)^{3}-2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(0)|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨-2,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0c87bb3-4a1e-5eee-8571-dbb4ddb80169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309599} {:answer/id #uuid "2577bf1f-28c9-55aa-9795-bd89ae4067d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309600} {:answer/id #uuid "6078f7b3-7f45-5ffc-aefb-f44c7c0a6b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309601} {:answer/id #uuid "85802045-332d-53cd-b049-6981cf1d9eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309602} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80450e-bdaa-5887-9466-525f3710bccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85802045-332d-53cd-b049-6981cf1d9eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309602} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc9b4f8-afc5-5ccc-9705-b13c094ffb66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "accdf36e-4deb-5652-9073-b1834a2b81f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121917" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}),2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(\\frac{\\pi }{8})|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(\\frac{\\pi }{8})| & =|⟨4,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4085bd9e-1247-5666-8815-aac532533d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309606} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ff216-38aa-57e4-b23f-625078696242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309607} {:answer/id #uuid "cecbd97b-7628-50d6-95f6-e7c18e8b9b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{5},-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309608} {:answer/id #uuid "07f65406-08e2-5687-9e82-865caec479cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡7,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309609} {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9729a40-615c-546c-828f-575525d28002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485b83d3-77da-50d6-9d88-1eeec6210db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-121919" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),1-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+1},-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨\\frac{3}{3(2)+1},-2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de9a797-f1ce-5b26-95ae-b407bc40c871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{4}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309613} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3707ab-3aff-547a-bdb9-82b7b3495816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309614} {:answer/id #uuid "5daab2e7-bcef-5e5a-8ce4-f57ae6e92922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}}{4}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309615} {:answer/id #uuid "7988b24a-09a9-50a6-a15e-dd284de2b3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309616} {:answer/id #uuid "99a38f52-cf6d-533e-9b66-10b423236b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3707ab-3aff-547a-bdb9-82b7b3495816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309614} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e63b100-734c-59f9-90da-29f98ac4158f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b79ce39-e3a6-5781-a9e4-71c93eee0b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-121920" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t-1},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t-1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨{2}^{1-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2(1)}{({1}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡2+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1bac3c8-2ed6-51cc-9baf-eb221e9d2c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309620} {:answer/id #uuid "1401b533-5bcd-5a5a-96c3-9d1a63744b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309621} {:answer/id #uuid "64163d55-81f8-5097-9ef7-f745ab03474c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309622} {:answer/id #uuid "db768bc7-f897-57d0-bb78-a3e5f24c0320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309623} {:answer/id #uuid "f004a71b-65e2-5c3e-b9ce-5a6e7c51c924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64163d55-81f8-5097-9ef7-f745ab03474c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309622} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad6b7c4b-9158-56fa-823b-ec85b2af869d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19cf5879-4ba8-59d0-8ef2-68769cbabfb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121926" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1,{e}^{2t+5}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5494cec3-fb51-5313-8219-063101216e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309627} {:answer/id #uuid "d5caa383-8750-5259-9428-99926ffe1d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-2,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309628} {:answer/id #uuid "02df329d-823b-533a-b41e-c19c5d2fcc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-2,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309629} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1e84bd-ed0e-5b62-8c16-c0c3d283b5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-8,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309630} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4f763b-f133-552b-b218-eec48441674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e4f763b-f133-552b-b218-eec48441674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309631} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d0f0d8-9c43-5c90-9ba3-276829ddff35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "052d683a-4534-50ca-9e0f-c6b7dbb2dbeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121932" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4-2{t}^{2},1-3{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,-9{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-9{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-4,-18(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a95e0f8-3d39-59a5-9c72-b0ebc49395ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a95e0f8-3d39-59a5-9c72-b0ebc49395ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309634} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b0b7bc9-1d77-53ae-ac81-456872b98360" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309633} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "698abd49-3d65-5f8c-89ce-e14e9f2f7da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "698abd49-3d65-5f8c-89ce-e14e9f2f7da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309636} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb5b59be-8248-5187-9914-1c566b5c07bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d914fdaf-5f44-5351-9a53-8c080fff8094" :question/math-academy-id "q-351106" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨9t-4,\\,2{t}^{3}+5⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9t-4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} {:answer/id #uuid "392a91b4-debd-575a-8ac2-0880734f81ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e493cd-5784-560a-899b-78f9ddc307aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642} :answer-field/id #uuid "57298f26-63eb-5cb0-b7bb-93bee04ed24c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8d865e3-b227-5cb9-adc4-9f5a7bac4f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-351111" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{e}^{3t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{e}^{3t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2974d91c-0100-55cd-b6ab-7d141f5084f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^t\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2974d91c-0100-55cd-b6ab-7d141f5084f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^t\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309645} :answer-field/id #uuid "37164766-c4cf-59f7-b1b4-3d979594054d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46a81839-4d09-5cd5-8e94-6d6e516beac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a81839-4d09-5cd5-8e94-6d6e516beac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309647} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb809df-90a5-50b6-9c7b-c93a541ffe49" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32aed3df-fecd-5116-8366-f0c14f8d968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-351116" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20cd8292-d4c3-58b2-ab94-2e6e7ae19a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20cd8292-d4c3-58b2-ab94-2e6e7ae19a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309650} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ab3c75-8fb1-5d76-9bce-4222c94b8a3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc3a10c-3e7d-5deb-993f-48d3fe1fbc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc3a10c-3e7d-5deb-993f-48d3fe1fbc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309652} :answer-field/id #uuid "44625857-1179-57b4-a112-ca1dc2ffc089" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6114abe5-977b-5d52-bf38-29f0303c45f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-351167" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨12(1)^{2},\\,-4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078f4a55-e197-5669-a3b5-12e60c824551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "078f4a55-e197-5669-a3b5-12e60c824551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309655} :answer-field/id #uuid "60418005-c94f-5ad6-90c0-f55fc85a1e6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea008953-1c5b-5269-bd0d-c70f8fe25f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-351176" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨3{t}^{2}+2t-1,-4t+5⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t+2,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6(1)+2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨8,-4⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $4\\sqrt{5}{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfa048a9-a209-5850-93f4-8d9828fdb018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfa048a9-a209-5850-93f4-8d9828fdb018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309658} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fe4d9b-596a-5bff-b6fe-56e1c63319d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65a5cb06-33e0-5346-a69b-49c202c1e37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24t^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a5cb06-33e0-5346-a69b-49c202c1e37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24t^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309660} :answer-field/id #uuid "59349d6a-dadb-575c-87aa-3c1c11d84554" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5acf3591-12db-5ea5-8cfd-578b4845491c" :question/math-academy-id "q-351377" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-8t,\\,8{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-8,\\,24{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c3c69-d0e1-5d52-bc83-c49503cbba24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52c3c69-d0e1-5d52-bc83-c49503cbba24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309663} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a477dc7-191c-5381-a8f8-1397a3567d93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b78ba92-0a9f-5bd9-8c0b-dd877b3cf2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b78ba92-0a9f-5bd9-8c0b-dd877b3cf2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309665} :answer-field/id #uuid "34faaa65-3a84-53e8-a08d-9c15c1d189b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d549734-6afe-524a-b538-81a8cf4b5cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-351381" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2}-2,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad7a68c8-d594-5864-b062-97c1d38643fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad7a68c8-d594-5864-b062-97c1d38643fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309668} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3452a71-bcdd-5c0e-bd81-a08b09523f3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e438b533-5029-56bc-b0d9-ed208b74b065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e438b533-5029-56bc-b0d9-ed208b74b065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309670} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99382ca-354f-578d-ac0f-d048b62edef6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ac73e4e-5042-5045-b159-6593d8be5960" :question/math-academy-id "q-351383" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f06bdd-c795-568b-a438-